---
import Proto from './_shared/Proto.astro';
import Header from './_shared/Header.astro';
import Footer from './_shared/Footer.astro';

// Real content from src/content/spelling-lists/phonics/soft-c-soft-g.md — used verbatim.
const words = ['city', 'ice', 'icy', 'giant', 'page', 'gym'];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is this the same as the c/k/ck spelling choice taught earlier?',
    a: "No — it goes the opposite direction. Earlier lessons teach how to spell the /k/ sound in the first place, including the choice between ck and k at the end of a word (covered by the CK, Double Letters, and TCH/DGE Word Endings Skill). This Skill starts from a c or g that is already written and asks what sound it makes, based on the letter that comes right after it.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the pattern still hold when an ending like -ing or -ed is added?',
    a: "Yes, as long as the ending itself starts with e, i, or y. Dance stays soft in dancing because -ing starts with i. Cage stays soft in caged because the silent e is dropped and replaced by the e that starts -ed. An ending starting with a, o, or a consonant wouldn't carry the softening the same way.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Do any other consonants change sound based on the letter that follows, the way c and g do?',
    a: 'Not in the same systematic way. C and G are the two letters in English spelling that regularly shift between a hard and a soft sound depending on whether e, i, or y comes next. Other letters can represent more than one sound (like s in sun versus s in rose), but not by this same before-e/i/y rule.',
  },
];

const relatedSkills = [
  { label: 'CK, Double Letters, and TCH/DGE Word Endings', href: '#' },
  { label: 'Long A Silent E Words', href: '#' },
  { label: 'Long I Silent E Words', href: '#' },
];
---

<Proto
  title="Soft C and Soft G — Exploration C"
  description="Design exploration C: skill detail page built from real Soft C and Soft G content."
>
  <Header currentPath="/design-explore/exploration-c/skill.html" />

  <main id="main">
    <div class="wrap page">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/design-explore/exploration-c/">Home</a>
        <span class="crumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <a href="/design-explore/exploration-c/#skills">Skills</a>
        <span class="crumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span aria-current="page">Soft C and Soft G</span>
      </nav>

      <div class="skill-layout">
        <article class="skill-main">
          <header class="skill-hero">
            <span class="eyebrow">Phonics · Grade 2</span>
            <h1>Soft C and Soft G</h1>
            <p class="skill-lede">
              Why <em>city</em> sounds different from <em>cat</em>, and <em>page</em> sounds
              different from <em>game</em>: c and g usually change from their hard sounds to soft
              ones when the next letter is e, i, or y.
            </p>
          </header>

          <section class="short-answer" aria-labelledby="short-answer-heading">
            <h2 id="short-answer-heading" class="visually-hidden">Short answer</h2>
            <p>
              Soft c and soft g are a real, teachable spelling pattern: when the letter right
              after c or g is e, i, or y, that c or g usually changes from its hard sound to a
              softer one. C changes from /k/ (as in <em>cat</em>) to /s/ (as in <em>city</em>). G
              changes from /g/ (as in <em>game</em>) to /j/ (as in <em>page</em>). The pattern is
              very reliable for c — soft c almost always shows up before e, i, or y. G is less
              reliable: high-frequency words like <em>get</em>, <em>give</em>, <em>girl</em>, and
              <em>gift</em> keep the hard /g/ sound even though g is followed by e or i.
            </p>
          </section>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              <em>C</em> and <em>g</em> each have a hard sound and a soft sound. Hard <em>c</em>
              says /k/, as in <em>cat</em>. Hard <em>g</em> says its own sound, /g/, as in
              <em>game</em>. But both letters change when the very next letter is <em>e</em>,
              <em>i</em>, or <em>y</em>: <em>c</em> softens to /s/, the sound at the start of
              <em>city</em> and <em>ice</em>. <em>G</em> softens to /j/, the sound at the start of
              <em>giant</em> and <em>page</em>. This is a genuine spelling pattern, useful for
              deciding how to spell a word, not just for reading — though, as the sections below
              show, it's considerably more dependable for c than for g.
            </p>

            <h3>How the Condition Works</h3>
            <p>
              Both letters change for the same reason, so the condition is worth stating once for
              both:
            </p>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">The next letter is…</th>
                    <th scope="col"><em>c</em> usually says</th>
                    <th scope="col"><em>g</em> usually says</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>e, i, or y</td>
                    <td>/s/ — soft (city, ice, icy)</td>
                    <td>/j/ — soft (giant, page, gym)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>a, o, u, or a consonant</td>
                    <td>/k/ — hard (cat, cot, cup)</td>
                    <td>/g/ — hard (game, got, gum)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p>
              Soft <em>c</em> and soft <em>g</em> almost always show up right before <em>e</em>,
              <em>i</em>, or <em>y</em> — that direction is dependable enough to trust. The reverse
              isn't guaranteed, though: seeing <em>e</em>, <em>i</em>, or <em>y</em> after a
              <em>c</em> or <em>g</em> doesn't always mean it will be soft. That gap matters much
              more for <em>g</em> than for <em>c</em>, which is why each letter gets its own look
              below.
            </p>

            <h3>Soft C: A Reliable Pattern</h3>
            <p>
              Soft c shows up in all three trigger positions: <em>city</em> (before <em>i</em>),
              <em>ice</em> (before <em>e</em>), <em>icy</em> (before <em>y</em>). Real exceptions
              among high-frequency words are rare — a child who applies "soft c before e, i, or y"
              can trust it in nearly every word they'll meet at this level.
            </p>

            <h3>Soft G: A Pattern With Real Exceptions</h3>
            <p>
              Soft g also shows up in all three positions: <em>giant</em> (before <em>i</em>),
              <em>page</em> (before <em>e</em>), <em>gym</em> (before <em>y</em>). But soft g is
              noticeably less dependable than soft c. Some of the most frequent words in a child's
              vocabulary keep the hard /g/ sound even though g is followed by e or i:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><em>get</em> — hard g, not "jet"</li>
              <li><em>give</em> — hard g, not "jive"</li>
              <li><em>girl</em> — hard g, not "jirl"</li>
              <li><em>gift</em> — hard g, not "jift"</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              This is the single most important thing to watch for with this pattern. A child who
              has just learned "g goes soft before e, i, or y" will often overapply it to these
              very familiar words — reading <em>get</em> as if it started like <em>gem</em>, or
              second-guessing how to spell it. Because <em>get</em>, <em>give</em>, <em>girl</em>,
              and <em>gift</em> are so common, it's worth naming this directly rather than letting
              a child discover it through repeated correction: soft g is a strong pattern, not an
              absolute one, and these four words are worth learning by sight as the exceptions to
              watch for.
            </p>

            <h3>What Should the Learner Notice?</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>
                The rule is about the letter written right after c or g — not about how the whole
                word sounds or feels.
              </li>
              <li>
                A silent letter still counts. In <em>ice</em> and <em>page</em>, the final e is
                silent — written but not pronounced — yet it's still the letter that makes the c
                or g in front of it soft. Long I Silent E Words and Long A Silent E Words cover why
                words like <em>ice</em> and <em>page</em> end in a silent e in the first place.
              </li>
              <li>
                One word can contain both the hard and soft version of the same letter. In
                <em>cage</em>, the first c is hard (before a) and the g is soft (before e). In
                <em>circus</em>, the first c is soft (before i) and the second c is hard (before
                u). Reading letter-by-letter, rather than guessing from the whole word, is what
                makes this pattern usable.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Why Bridge Isn't Spelled "Brige"</h3>
            <p>
              Soft g explains a spelling choice from another Skill. Words like <em>bridge</em>,
              <em>badge</em>, and <em>edge</em> need the /j/ sound right after a short vowel — but
              they don't simply add e after the g (as in <em>page</em>), because adding a silent e
              after a single consonant is usually the signal that turns a short vowel into a long
              one (the pattern behind <em>page</em>, <em>cake</em>, and the rest of the Silent E
              family). Spelling it "brige" would look, by that usual pattern, like it should rhyme
              with a long i (as in <em>ride</em>), not the short i in <em>bridge</em>. English uses
              <em>dge</em> instead, which keeps the /j/ sound without disturbing the vowel before
              it. The CK, Double Letters, and TCH/DGE Word Endings Skill covers this ending in
              full; soft g is what makes the workaround necessary in the first place.
            </p>

            <h3>A Routine for Practicing Soft C and Soft G</h3>
            <ol>
              <li>Say the word aloud and listen for /s/, /j/, /k/, or /g/ at the target spot.</li>
              <li>Look at (or predict) the letter right after c or g.</li>
              <li>
                Match the sound to the letter: e, i, or y usually means soft; a, o, u, or a
                consonant usually means hard.
              </li>
              <li>
                Write the word, checking that the letter chosen for the sound is actually c or g
                rather than s, k, or j.
              </li>
              <li>
                For get, give, girl, and gift, skip the letter check and rely on the word itself
                being familiar by sight.
              </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              When a child spells a soft-sound word with the wrong letter — writing <em>jem</em>
              for <em>gem</em>, or <em>sirkus</em> for <em>circus</em> — resist supplying the
              correct spelling right away. Ask what letter usually comes after a soft c or soft g,
              and let the child check the word against that condition themselves, rather than
              simply being told the fix.
            </p>

            <h3>Signs This Pattern Is Becoming Secure</h3>
            <p>
              Signs the pattern is becoming secure include: predicting whether an unfamiliar c or
              g will be hard or soft by checking the next letter; reading and spelling <em
                >get</em
              >, <em>give</em>, <em>girl</em>, and <em>gift</em> correctly without hesitating over
              the g; noticing both sounds inside a single word like <em>cage</em> or <em
                >circus</em
              >; and explaining, in their own words, why <em>bridge</em> uses <em>dge</em> instead of
              a plain g.
            </p>
          </div>

          <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
            <h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
            <div class="faq-list">
              {
                faqs.map((item, i) => (
                  <details class="faq-item" open={i === 0}>
                    <summary>{item.q}</summary>
                    <p>{item.a}</p>
                  </details>
                ))
              }
            </div>
          </section>
        </article>

        <aside class="skill-side" aria-labelledby="wordlist-heading">
          <div class="wordlist-card">
            <h2 id="wordlist-heading">This Skill's words</h2>
            <ul class="wordlist">
              {
                words.map((word) => (
                  <li>
                    <button type="button" class="wordlist-audio" aria-label={`Play ${word}`}>
                      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                        <path d="M4 9v6h4l5 5V4L8 9H4z" fill="currentColor" />
                      </svg>
                    </button>
                    <span class="wordlist-word">{word}</span>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
            <a
              class="btn btn-practice wordlist-cta"
              href="/design-explore/exploration-c/practice.html">Practice these 6 words</a
            >
          </div>

          <nav class="related-card" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
            <h2 id="related-heading">Related skills</h2>
            <ul class="related-list">
              {
                relatedSkills.map((skill) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href={skill.href}>{skill.label}</a>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />

  <style>
    .page {
      padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
    }

    .breadcrumbs {
      display: flex;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      align-items: center;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: var(--ink-faint);
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
    .breadcrumbs a {
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: 600;
      border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    }
    .breadcrumbs a:hover {
      color: var(--skill-strong);
      border-bottom-color: var(--skill-vivid);
    }
    .crumb-sep {
      color: var(--skill-vivid);
      opacity: 0.55;
    }
    .breadcrumbs [aria-current='page'] {
      color: var(--ink);
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .skill-layout {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 300px;
      gap: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .skill-main {
      min-width: 0;
    }

    .skill-hero {
      max-width: 68ch;
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      display: inline-block;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      background: var(--skill);
      color: var(--skill-ink);
      padding: 0.28rem 0.7rem;
      border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    .skill-hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.8vw, 2.5rem);
      margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
    }

    .skill-lede {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      line-height: 1.65;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
    }

    .short-answer {
      background: var(--canvas-raised);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-left: 4px solid var(--skill-strong);
      border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;
      padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    }

    .short-answer p {
      font-size: 0.96rem;
      line-height: 1.65;
      color: var(--ink);
      margin: 0;
    }

